    "# <img src=\"tutorial-icons/theory.png\" alt=\"Theory\" width=\"44\" style=\"vertical-align:middle; margin-right:10px;\"> Theory Foundation: Trees, Impurity, Pruning, and Boosting\n",
    "\n",
    "A decision tree partitions feature space into regions and predicts a constant class or value inside each terminal region. For classification, a candidate split is judged by how much it reduces node impurity. Two common impurity measures are\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "Gini(t)=1-\\sum_{k=1}^{K}\\hat p_{k\\mid t}^2,\n",
    "\\qquad\n",
    "Entropy(t)=-\\sum_{k=1}^{K}\\hat p_{k\\mid t}\\log \\hat p_{k\\mid t}.\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "The split improvement is parent impurity minus the weighted impurity of the children. Greedy splitting chooses the best immediate improvement; it does not search every possible tree.\n",
    "\n",
    "Deep trees have low training bias but high variance. Cost-complexity pruning balances fit and size:\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "R_\\alpha(T)=R(T)+\\alpha |T|,\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "where $|T|$ is the number of terminal nodes. Larger $\\alpha$ favors simpler trees. Tree depth, minimum leaf size, and pruning must be selected inside training data, because using test performance to choose the tree spends the test set.\n",
    "\n",
    "Boosting builds an additive model sequentially,\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "F_M(x)=F_0(x)+\\sum_{m=1}^{M}\\eta\\,h_m(x),\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "where each new weak learner $h_m$ focuses on errors or gradients left by the current ensemble and $\\eta$ is the learning rate. Gradient boosting approximates functional gradient descent on a chosen loss. More trees can reduce bias but also increase computation and overfitting risk; a smaller learning rate usually requires more trees.\n",
    "\n",
    "Single trees provide explicit decision paths but can be unstable. Boosted ensembles often predict better but distribute behavior across many trees, making global explanation harder. Feature importance is not causality and can be biased by feature structure. Error analysis, stability, calibration, and subgroup performance remain necessary.\n",
    "\n",
    "### Questions you should be ready to answer\n",
    "\n",
    "- What does impurity measure, and why does a split reduce it?\n",
    "- Why is the tree-growing procedure greedy?\n",
    "- What role does $\\alpha$ play in pruning?\n",
    "- How is boosting different from fitting independent trees and averaging them?"
   ]
  },
