    "# Level 1 — Required Core\n",
    "\n",
    "# Part 1: Define the Classification Problem\n",
    "\n",
    "In `mushrooms.csv`, the response column is `class`:\n",
    "\n",
    "- `e` = edible;\n",
    "- `p` = poisonous.\n",
    "\n",
    "The remaining columns describe categorical characteristics such as cap shape, odor, gill size, stalk properties, population, and habitat.\n",
    "\n",
    "A classification tree repeatedly divides the represented predictor space. Each terminal leaf estimates class proportions from the training observations that reach that leaf.\n",
    "\n",
    "## <img src=\"tutorial-icons/theory.png\" alt=\"Theory\" width=\"36\" style=\"vertical-align:middle; margin-right:9px;\"> Theory: Impurity and Splits\n",
    "\n",
    "For two classes, Gini impurity can be written as:\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "G = 1-\\sum_{k=1}^{2}\\widehat{p}_k^2.\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "Entropy is:\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "H=-\\sum_{k=1}^{2}\\widehat{p}_k\\log_2(\\widehat{p}_k).\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "Both are small when a node is nearly pure. A useful split produces child nodes with lower weighted impurity than the parent.\n"
   ]
  },
