    "# Part 4: Control Complexity With Pruning\n",
    "\n",
    "A fully grown tree can memorize small irregular regions. ISLP describes cost-complexity pruning as balancing training fit against the number of terminal nodes.\n",
    "\n",
    "In scikit-learn, `ccp_alpha` controls that penalty. Larger values generally produce smaller trees. The value must be chosen using the training data, not the test set.\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "theory-t7-01",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## <img src=\"tutorial-icons/theory.png\" alt=\"Theory\" width=\"36\" style=\"vertical-align:middle; margin-right:9px;\"> Theory: Pruning Balances Fit and Tree Size\n",
    "\n",
    "An unrestricted tree can keep splitting until it captures small training irregularities. Cost-complexity pruning adds a penalty for additional leaves. The penalty value must be selected within the training data because choosing it from test performance would tune the tree to the final evaluation rows.\n",
    "\n",
    "### Deeper explanation\n",
    "\n",
    "The pruning path produces nested subtrees indexed by $\\alpha$. Cross-validation estimates which complexity generalizes, and a one-standard-error rule may choose a smaller tree whose score is statistically indistinguishable from the minimum. Pruning improves stability but does not guarantee stable split variables; bootstrap refits can reveal how often important branches change.\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "00d8b2c2",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## <img src=\"tutorial-icons/claude_task.png\" alt=\"Claude Task\" width=\"36\" style=\"vertical-align:middle; margin-right:9px;\"> Claude Coding Task 4: Select a Pruned Tree With Cross-Validation\n",
    "\n",
    "```text\n",
    "Using only X_train and y_train:\n",
