    "# Part 5: From One Tree to an Ensemble and General Boosting\n",
    "\n",
    "An **ensemble** combines many models into one prediction.\n",
    "\n",
    "- **Bagging** fits trees on bootstrap samples and aggregates their predictions.\n",
    "- **Random forests** add random predictor selection to reduce similarity among trees.\n",
    "- **Boosting** grows trees sequentially. Each new weak tree focuses on structure the current ensemble has not captured well.\n",
    "\n",
    "ISLP emphasizes three boosting controls:\n",
    "\n",
    "- the number of trees $B$;\n",
    "- the shrinkage or learning-rate parameter $\\lambda$;\n",
    "- the depth $d$ of each tree.\n",
    "\n",
    "A smaller learning rate makes each tree a smaller correction and usually requires more trees. More trees are not automatically better; boosting can eventually overfit.\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "theory-t7-02",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## <img src=\"tutorial-icons/theory.png\" alt=\"Theory\" width=\"36\" style=\"vertical-align:middle; margin-right:9px;\"> Theory: Boosting Learns Sequentially\n",
    "\n",
    "Bagging builds many trees independently and averages them. Boosting is different: each new weak tree is added to improve the current ensemble. Learning rate controls the size of each contribution, tree depth controls the interactions each learner can represent, and the number of trees controls how many updates are made.\n",
    "\n",
    "### Deeper explanation\n",
    "\n",
    "At iteration $m$, gradient boosting fits $h_m$ to the negative gradient of the loss evaluated at current predictions. For squared error, these are residuals. The learning rate $\\eta$, tree depth, and number of iterations interact: shallow trees model low-order interactions, small $\\eta$ makes incremental corrections, and too many iterations can fit noise. Validation or early stopping is needed.\n"
   ]
  },
