   "source": [
    "# AI for Social Good: Accuracy, Fairness, and Consequence Must Be Evaluated Together\n",
    "\n",
    "This notebook’s mushroom example makes one point very clear: **high predictive accuracy is not permission to act** when the cost of an error is severe. The earlier course extended the same idea to public and fairness-sensitive decisions, where different groups can experience different false-positive and false-negative rates.\n",
    "\n",
    "Decision trees are attractive because their splits are visible, but visibility alone does not make a decision fair. Boosted trees may improve accuracy while making the final rule harder to explain. In a consequential application, ask:\n",
    "\n",
    "- Which inputs are allowed, and which variables act as proxies for protected or structural characteristics?\n",
    "- Which groups experience the worst error, not only the average error?\n",
    "- Would minimizing the maximum group loss lead to a different model than maximizing overall accuracy?\n",
    "- Which fairness definition fits the real harm: equal error, false-positive parity, false-negative parity, worst-group performance, or something else?\n",
    "- Is the model being used to assist an expert, prioritize review, or make the final decision?\n",
    "\n",
    "For the mushroom task, verified human identification remains necessary. For public-interest classification, affected communities and domain experts are equally necessary to define what an acceptable error trade-off means.\n",
    "\n",
    "> **Social-good principle:** Fairness is not a single metric, and accuracy is not authority. The consequence and distribution of errors determine what must be checked.\n"
   ]
  },
