   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "theory-t7-03",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## <img src=\"tutorial-icons/theory.png\" alt=\"Theory\" width=\"36\" style=\"vertical-align:middle; margin-right:9px;\"> Theory: XGBoost Extends the Same Boosting Theory\n",
    "\n",
    "XGBoost adds regularization, sampling controls, efficient split search, and strong software engineering to gradient boosting. These features can improve speed and control complexity, but they do not remove the need to define the costly error, tune with training data, and evaluate on untouched rows.\n",
    "\n",
    "### Deeper explanation\n",
    "\n",
    "XGBoost uses first- and second-order loss information and adds explicit tree-complexity penalties. A simplified objective is $\\sum_i L(y_i,\\hat y_i)+\\sum_m\\Omega(h_m)$. Regularization, row/column subsampling, and efficient split search improve control and computation, but the model remains a boosted tree ensemble whose tuning must stay inside training data.\n"
   ]
  },
